{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","count":22,"questions":[{"id":51281,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Phosphorous reacts with chlorine gas to give a colourless liquid, which fumes in moist air to produce $\\mathrm{HCl}$ and","options":["$\\mathrm{POCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FCTgFZHf3B33hXkqJ0R2dxqipc0vrxIGwE7NV0pSJ3_0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FCTgFZHf3B33hXkqJ0R2dxqipc0vrxIGwE7NV0pSJ3_0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FCTgFZHf3B33hXkqJ0R2dxqipc0vrxIGwE7NV0pSJ3_0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213e0727a44d6769c4a"},{"id":51282,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A filter paper soaked in salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> turns brown when exposed to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>HNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> vapor. The salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KCl</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KBr</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>KI</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>As, a filter paper soaked in salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>X</mtext></math>turns brown when exposed to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>H</mtext><mtext>N</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>3</mn></msub></math> vapour, then salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>X</mtext></math> must be a strong reducing agent which will reduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>H</mtext><mtext>N</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>3</mn></msub></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>N</mtext><msub><mtext>O</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub></math> (brown gas).</p>\n\n<p>Among the given salt, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>K</mtext><mtext>I</mtext></math> is the strongest reducing agent. Thus, salt <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>X</mtext></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>K</mtext><mtext>I</mtext></math>.</p>\n\n<p>undefined<br />\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3fe3ae5fb9d3549625f"},{"id":51283,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following compounds, the one which does NOT produce nitrogen gas upon heating is","options":["$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$","$\\mathrm{NaN}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}$","$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2}\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)$"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{N}_{2}+\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}2 \\mathrm{NaN}_{3} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Na}+3 \\mathrm{~N}_{2}\\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{NO}_{2} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{N}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{4}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4} \\longrightarrow 2 \\mathrm{NH}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213061188975ad2410e","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51284,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The molecule which is NOT hydrolysed by water at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is","options":["$\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SiCl}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Due to steric hinderance $\\mathrm{SF}_{6}$ is not hydrolysed by $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ at $25^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21365ee92bb4aa21dc5"},{"id":51285,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The complete hydrolysis of $\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ results in the formation of","options":["$\\mathrm{XeO}_{2} \\mathrm{~F}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{XeOF}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{XeO}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{XeO}_{2}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\longrightarrow \\underset{\\text { white explosive compound }}{\\mathrm{XeO}_{3}}+6 \\mathrm{HF}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21363b2addd7e947e3c"},{"id":51286,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"White phosphorous catches fire in air to produce dense white fumes. This is due to the formation of","options":["$\\mathrm{P}_{4} \\mathrm{O}_{10}$","$\\mathrm{PH}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{3}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Theoritical","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213a4b35228b3ca8c28","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51287,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>, the element (s) that produce (s) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> on reaction with hot conc. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is/are","options":["Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math>","Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math>","Only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>The reactions between given elements, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> with conc. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> are as follows:</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi>CO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Thus, all the given elements produces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> on reaction with hot concentration.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68b1966d5d427c61b33"},{"id":51288,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The hybridisation of xenon atom in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>The hybridisation of any compound can be calculated as,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math><br />\n[Number of Valence electrons at central atom</p>\n\n<p>+ Number of mono valent atoms attached to central atom+total negative charge on the molecule-total positive charge on the molecule]</p>\n\n<p>X = number of hybrid orbitals</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> The hybridisation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>s</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3fe67f7b809ffcd3f9e"},{"id":51289,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The oxoacid of phosphorus having the strongest reducing property is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>The extent of reducing property is determined by the oxidation state of central atom. More is the oxidation state lesser will be the reducing property. The oxidation states of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> in given oxoacids are as follows:</p>\n\n<p>(i) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>(ii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>(iii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>(iv) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>As the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> is least in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>PO</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> Thus, it has the strongest reducing property.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68b81f267e8216cfd64"},{"id":51290,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The major products obtained in the reaction of oxalic acid with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ upon heating are","options":["$\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","$\\mathrm{CO}, \\mathrm{SO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}, \\mathrm{CO}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$","$\\mathrm{HCOOH}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~S}, \\mathrm{CO}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Conc. sulphuric acid is a strong dehydrating agent. Upon reaction with oxalic acid at elevated temperature, it removes water, carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide as\n\\(\\underset{\\text{hydrated}}{\\mathrm{HOOC}}-\\underset{\\text{oxalic}}{\\mathrm{COOH}} .\\underset{\\text{acid}}{2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}+\\underset{\\text{hot, conc.}}{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}} \\rightarrow \\underset{\\text{water}}{3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}+ \\underset{\\text{carbon monoxide}}{\\mathrm{CO}}+\\underset{\\text{carbon dioxide}}{\\mathrm{CO}_{2}}\\)","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21330a658d07fbf8558"},{"id":51291,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"When $262 \\mathrm{~g}$ of xenon (atomic mass $=131$ ) reacted completely with $152 \\mathrm{~g}$ of fluorine (atomic mass $=19$ ), a mixture of $\\mathrm{XeF}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ was produced. The molar ratio $\\mathrm{XeF}_{2}: \\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ is :","options":["1: 2","1: 4","$\\mathrm1: 1$","1: 3"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Xe}+\\mathrm{F}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{XeF}_{2}+\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}\\frac{262}{131} \\quad \\frac{152}{38}$ a mole $\\mathrm{b}$ mole $=2$ mole 4 mole Let a mole $\\mathrm{XeF}_{2}$ form and $\\mathrm{b}$ mole $\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ form Apply POAC  \\begin{array}{ll}\\mathrm{a} \\times 1+\\mathrm{b} \\times 1=2 \\\\ 2 \\mathrm{a}+6 \\mathrm{~b}=8\\end{array}  After solving eq. $(1) \\&(2)\\mathrm{a}=1$ mole $\\& \\mathrm{~b}=1 \\mathrm{~mole}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2132705d5a0c7d2f392"},{"id":51292,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"When traded with conc. $\\mathrm{HCl}_{2} \\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ yields gas $(\\mathrm{X})$ which further reacts with $\\mathrm{Ca}\\left(\\mathrm{OH}_{2}\\right)$ to generate $\\mathrm{a}$ white solid (Y) reacts with dil. HCl to produce the same gas $X$. the solid $Y$ is","options":["$\\mathrm{CaO}$","$\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{Ca}(\\mathrm{OCl}) \\mathrm{Cl}$","$\\mathrm{CaCO}_{3}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FQJlSSCwcMECQUc3Dtb2alP0tGH1R2X9Nq8JXdwrprdQ.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQJlSSCwcMECQUc3Dtb2alP0tGH1R2X9Nq8JXdwrprdQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQJlSSCwcMECQUc3Dtb2alP0tGH1R2X9Nq8JXdwrprdQ.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2137408d36d8043e51a"},{"id":51293,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","exa