></msub></math> produces another chromium compound along with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> gas. The change of the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math> in the reaction is","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>8</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<p>The heating of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math> produces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> compound along with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mover><mo>⟶</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mover><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Let the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math></p>\n\n<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>Cr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>=</mo></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo></math> The change of the oxidation state of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cr</mi></math> in the reaction is from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da694fd6e76599ce75374"},{"id":51300,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ of oxoacids of chlorine in water follows the order","options":["$\\mathrm{HClO} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{HClO}_{4} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}$","$\\mathrm{HClO}_{4} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}$","$\\mathrm{HClO}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} \\lt  \\mathrm{HClO}_{4}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Acidic strength of acid is\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{HClO}_{4}>\\mathrm{HClO}_{3}>\\mathrm{HClO}_{2}>\\mathrm{HClO} \\\\\n{\\left[\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right] \\uparrow \\mathrm{ka} \\uparrow \\mathrm{p}^{\\mathrm{ka}} \\downarrow} \\\\\n\\therefore \\mathrm{p}^{\\mathrm{k}_{\\mathrm{a}}} \\text { order } \\mathrm{HClO}_{4} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{3} < \\mathrm{HClO}_{2} < \\mathrm{HClO}\n\\end{array}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b8eff750e53d2813"},{"id":51301,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The role of haemoglobin is to<br />\n ","options":["store oxygen in muscles","transport oxygen to different parts of the body","convert <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mi>O</mi></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","convert <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> into carbonic acid"],"answer":1,"solution":"The role of haemoglobin is to transport oxygen from lungs or gills to different parts of the body. There it releases the oxygen to permit aerobic respiration to provide energy to power the functions of the organism in the process called metabolism.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SA)","_marksId":"614da3fefde45e6c28397617"},{"id":51302,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"p Block Elements (Group 15, 16, 17 & 18)","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"$\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ hydrolyses to give an oxide. The structure of $\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}$ and the oxide, respectively, are-","options":["octahedral and tetrahedral","distorted octahedral and pyramidal","octahedral and pyramidal","distorted octahedral and tetrahedral"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{ll}\\frac{\\mathrm{XeF}_{6}}{\\downarrow}+3 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow & \\frac{\\mathrm{XeO}_{3}}{\\downarrow}+6 \\mathrm{HF} \\\\ \\mathrm{Sp}^{3} \\mathrm{~d}^{3} \\text { Hybridisation } & \\mathrm{Sp}^{3} \\text { Hybridisation } \\\\ \\text { (distorted octahedral) } & \\text { (Pyramidal) }\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213f9267526fc433c68"}]}